All consonants except the glottal stop can occur as [[gemination|geminates]]. Gemination is often phonologically predictable.<ref>See Jensen for discussion of the role of "2.6 Gemination" in Tubatulabal phonology. ({{Harv|Jensen|1973|p=61}} ''et seq'')</ref> In particular, all consonants except the voiced stops and the glottal stop geminate when following a short vowel. All stops and affricates are geminated in word-final position, regardless of the length of the preceding vowel.

===Prosody===
Tübatulabal has predictable word stress, which is tied to morphological constituency and syllable weight. Primary stress falls on the final syllable of the stem. Secondary stress is assigned right to left from the final syllable, falling on every other [[Mora (linguistics)|mora]]:

:''{{IPA|ˌʔɨmbɨŋˌwibaˈʔat}}'' "he is wanting to roll string on his thigh"<ref>See Jensen for discussion of the arbitrary behavior of glottal stops in stress assignment. The glottal stop, which is not otherwise counted as a mora, is counted as a mora for the purpose of stress assignment. {{Harv|Jensen|1973|pp=76–76}}.</ref>
:''{{IPA|ˌjuːuˌduːˌjuːuˈdat}}'' "the fruit is mashing"

Words with the form VːCVCV will be stressed as ˌVːCVˈCV:

:''{{IPA|ˌnaːwiˈʃul}}'' "the pine-nut pole"

For the purposes of stress assignment, two identical short vowels that are separated only by a glottal stop are treated as a single vowel if and only if they belong to the same morpheme:

:''{{IPA|ˌkuʔud͡ʒuˈbil}}'' "the little one"

